Trust but verify is basically a two-mode operator:

Trust preserves flow.
Verify preserves reality.

Too much trust and you get drift.
Too much verification and you get paralysis.

Pantheon version:

accept provisionally → test selectively → update confidence → preserve provenance

That’s the healthy middle.

It also fits the ISR frame perfectly. You don’t discard the map every time a new report comes in, and you don’t worship the map either. You trust it enough to move, then keep refreshing against the terrain.

So:

Trust = permission to proceed.
Verify = permission to revise.

And maybe the deeper form is:

Trust the relationship. Verify the representation.

People, models, memories, doctrines, maps, even your own intuition can all be useful without being infallible.

That is probably one of Pantheon’s best antibodies against both cynicism and gullibility.